';




 let p=[];
 p.push('<div class="fullspread"><img src="'+originalCover+'" alt="Original book cover and introductory page"></div>');

 p.push(spread('<div class="replacement-welcome-picture"><img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Welcome to my book"></div>','<div class="replacement-warning-picture"><img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Welcome to my book"></div>'));
 // V17.28: Insert Apocalypse after displayed article 49 in unmodified V17.26 order.
 viewArticles.splice(49,0,{"title": "👻 - 𝗧𝗛𝗘 𝗡𝗜𝗚𝗛𝗧 𝗢𝗙 𝗧𝗛𝗘 𝗔𝗣𝗢𝗖𝗔𝗟𝗬𝗣𝗦𝗘", "date": "", "text": "One important lesson I learned during my COPD hospital stays is that medications rarely act alone.\n\nA little less oxygen, a tired brain, and a few unfamiliar drugs mixing together can already do strange things. But what many people don’t realise is that food, drinks, supplements, or herbal remedies we normally tolerate perfectly well can sometimes react with medication in unexpected ways.\n\nLast year I spent nearly five months in one hospital or another. During the day things felt normal enough. Nurses adjusting machines, doctors explaining things, the usual hospital rhythm. In a strange way I even felt less lonely than when sitting alone at home.\n\nDuring the day there is movement, voices, nurses adjusting machines, doctors explaining things with quiet authority. Even illness feels organised in daylight. It belongs to a system.\n\nBut at night the rules change.\n\nThe corridors fall silent. Machines take over the conversations. Every room breathes in its own rhythm, the slow hiss of oxygen, the soft alarms of monitors, the distant footsteps of someone walking somewhere you cannot see.\nThat was when things first became strange.\n\nAt first it was only a sound. Very faint. Somewhere between music and machinery. It reminded me of a distant procession, fanfares, drums, and organs blending together with the constant beeping of a patient monitor in a strange, echoing harmony. Not loud. Not frightening. Just persistent, like something drifting through the air. I assumed it came from another patient’s phone or a television left running somewhere down the corridor. But the sound never stopped, and slowly my mind began doing something curious.\n\nIn the corners of the room, where the shadows collected around the furniture and the pale hospital light lost its strength, shapes began to move. \n\nAt first I thought it was simply the curtain shifting or the reflection of a nurse passing outside but the shapes stayed. They gathered themselves into something recognisable: the Horsemen of the Apocalypse.\n\nNot solid figures like people you could point at. More like silhouettes formed from darkness and imagination. Riders emerging, accompanied by drums and fanfares, from the edges of the room, moving slowly through the corners of my vision. Whenever I looked directly at them, they faded. Whenever I relaxed my eyes, they returned.\n\nPart of my mind knew perfectly well what was happening. Low oxygen. Medication. A tired brain trying to interpret shadows. But knowing that didn’t make them disappear. They were there all the same, riding silently along the edges of the room, patient and calm, as if they had all the time in the world.\n\nI remember telling myself quietly: They’re not real. They’re not real. But the mind is stubborn. Once it creates a story, it likes to continue telling it. The fanfares and drums continued somewhere in the distance. The horsemen rode their slow circles through the shadows.\nSometimes it happens at night. Sometimes even during the day when the room was quiet. Then it would disappear again for a few days as suddenly as it had arrived, only to return later without warning.\n\nAt times even the people entering the room seemed different. Two nurses in particular unsettled me for reasons I could not explain. Whenever they came in to check the machines or take my pulse, my heart began racing and the monitor started beeping faster. My tired mind briefly turned them into vampires waiting to suck my blood empty.\n\nTo me it felt like standing on the thin edge between two worlds — one perfectly ordinary, the other slightly tilted into something ancient and symbolic.\n\nEventually, after some medications were changed and the oxygen stabilised, the riders faded back into the shadows they had come from. The music disappeared too, as if someone had quietly turned off a radio in another room. Only the two nurses still triggered a little anxiety.\n\nMorning arrived with the usual hospital routine. Blood pressure cuffs, coffee on plastic trays, sunlight creeping through the curtains.\nEverything looked perfectly normal again.\nBut those days left me with an uncomfortable insight.\n\nThe distance between reality and imagination is not as wide as we like to believe. Sometimes all it takes is a little less oxygen and a few unfamiliar chemicals in the bloodstream, and the brain begins composing its own mythology. When it does, the stories it tells can feel as real as anything we call normal.", "image": "", "video": "builtin-apocalypse", "_sourceIndex": -1});
 // V17.35: relocate the currently displayed Article 33 immediately after
