 // V17.57: Article 2 full text, original picture preserved.
 const article2Full=viewArticles.find(a=>/Questions When COPD Moved In/i.test(a.title||""));
 if(article2Full){article2Full.text="🤔 𝗤𝗨𝗘𝗦𝗧𝗜𝗢𝗡𝗦 𝗪𝗛𝗘𝗡 𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗 𝗠𝗢𝗩𝗘𝗗 𝗜𝗡\n     (Uninvited. Didn’t even take its shoes off)\n\nI didn’t plan on writing this.\nI also didn’t plan on getting COPD, so clearly planning isn’t my strongest skill.\n\nThis isn’t medical advice, a success story, or a miracle-cure thread. It’s simply a collection of the questions I asked when COPD turned up unannounced and started rearranging the furniture.\n\nIf you’re newly diagnosed, you’ll recognise a few of them. If you’ve been around longer, you may nod and think: *“Yes… that one at 3 a.m.”*\n\nEither way — welcome. ❤️\n\n𝟭. 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁’𝘀 𝗺𝘆 𝗹𝗶𝗳𝗲 𝗲𝘅𝗽𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗮𝗻𝗰𝘆?\nFirst thought. Obviously.\nTyped into Google at a stupid hour.\nClosed the browser five minutes later feeling both immortal *and* already dead.\nVery efficient.\n\n𝟮. 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗲𝘅𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗹𝘆 𝗶𝘀 𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗?\nBecause the doctor said the word, handed me a leaflet, and sent me home like I’d just been given Chinese instructions for a toaster.\nStill haven’t deciphered them.\n\nCOPD support groups — and a bit of help from a friend — explained it far better than the leaflet ever did.\n\n𝟯. 𝗜𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗲 𝗮 𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗲?\nNO.\n\nBut such a clean, brutal no was simply not imaginable, so I asked hopefully.\n\nGoogle — like a cult leader — was more than happy to help and generate any amount of hope you require at the click of a button and $9.99 plus tax. Apparently everything is curable if you drink the right tea, click the right link, send some money, and sell your soul.\n\nHope dies last — but it felt rude not to give it a proper chance.\n\nI then moved straight on to the five stages of grief.\nSkipped denial.\nLanded somewhere between sarcasm and resignation.\n\n𝟰. 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗳𝗮𝘀𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝗴𝗼𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝘁𝗼 𝗴𝗲𝘁 𝘄𝗼𝗿𝘀𝗲?\nAlso known as: should I stop buying green bananas, or can I still plan for… well, anything?\n\nThe honest answer is nobody knows.\nGoogle panics enthusiastically and recommends tarot readers.\nDoctors shrug professionally.\n\nSo I live somewhere between *“I’m Superman, I’m invincible”* and *“Panic — I’m dying and I haven’t paid my rent yet.”*\n\n𝟱. 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗜 𝗲𝗻𝗱 𝘂𝗽 𝗼𝗻 𝗼𝘅𝘆𝗴𝗲𝗻, 𝗶𝗻 𝗮 𝘄𝗵𝗲𝗲𝗹𝗰𝗵𝗮𝗶𝗿, 𝗼𝗿 𝗽𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗮𝗻𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗹𝘆 𝗵𝗼𝗿𝗶𝘇𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗹?\nCue the full horror slideshow.\nOxygen tanks. Tubes. Machines that beep at night just to remind you they exist.\nWheelchairs that move at a speed best described as a strongly opinionated snail.\n\nTurns out COPD doesn’t follow a script — and Google definitely shouldn’t be the director.\n\n𝟲. 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗰𝗮𝗻 𝗜 𝗮𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆 𝗱𝗼 𝗮𝗯𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝗶𝘁?\nThis is when panic gets bored and leaves.\nI stay — with a notebook and unrealistic expectations.\n\nI was hoping for one simple trick. A magic inhaler. Some forbidden breathing technique discovered by monks in the Himalayas.\n\nInstead: I got a list of small, sensible, deeply unsexy things.\n\nManaging COPD turns out to be less about fixing anything and more about learning how to coexist with a body that has quietly stopped negotiating.\n\n𝟳. 𝗜𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝗺𝘆 𝗳𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁?\nMost likely it is, in one way or another. Smoking, work, pollution, or a lifetime of decisions made with great confidence and absolutely no foresight — but guilt turned out to be heavy, useless, and surprisingly bad for my breathing, so I retired from that emotion early on. \n\n𝟴. 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗼 𝗜 𝗱𝗲𝗮𝗹 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 𝗯𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗵𝗹𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗻𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗽𝗮𝗻𝗶𝗰?\nBreathlessness is bad enough. Panic is the unnecessary sequel nobody asked for.\n\nI keep telling myself “don’t panic, don’t panic” — and then... I panic. Immediately. With enthusiasm.\n\nIf panic hits, I usually place my hand gently on my heart and take five slow, deep breaths. My heartbeat slows down and my mind becomes quieter. Feels like it reconnects something. Brain to body… or maybe something else. Hard to explain, but it pulls me back. Out of the head, back into the moment.  \n\nUnderstanding what’s happening doesn’t fix my lungs, but it does stop my brain from setting off the fire alarm every time I climb the stairs. That helps. A bit.\n\n𝟵. 𝗖𝗮𝗻 𝗜 𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗹𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗮 𝗻𝗼𝗿𝗺𝗮𝗹 (𝗰𝗵𝗮𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗰) 𝗹𝗶𝗳𝗲❓\nI probably won’t attend the Full Moon Party, play with fire, dance till sunrise or casually lose three days and a flip-flop.\n\nBut yes — I can still live. Just at a different speed (5.3 km/h if I use the wheelchair), with more planning and fewer life choices that end with “How did I get here?”\n\nThe chaos didn’t disappear. It just got more selective. Still me, just slightly less flammable.\n\n𝟭𝟬. 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗼 𝗜 𝗸𝗲𝗲𝗽 𝗺𝘆 𝗵𝗲𝗮𝗱 𝗳𝗿𝗼𝗺 𝗰𝗼𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲𝘁𝗲𝗹𝘆 𝗳𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗮𝗽𝗮𝗿𝘁?\nOnce the body stabilises a bit, the mind finally gets its turn — replaying conversations, rewriting the future, and calling it *“helpful.”*\n\nWhat worked wasn’t forced positivity or pretending everything’s fine, but honesty (how ever hard that might be), humour, learning what’s actually happening, talking to people who get it, and accepting that some days are simply heavier.\n\nStaying sane with COPD isn’t about being strong all the time. It’s about knowing when to slow down, sit down, breathe, and let the day be what it is.\n\n𝗖𝗟𝗢𝗦𝗜𝗡𝗚\nI won’t die from COPD, but I live with it.\nLike everyone else, I’ll die because I was born.\n\nCOPD came along uninvited and changed the pace, but it didn’t take away who I am.\nThe questions keep changing, the answers evolve — and none of us are doing this alone 💜";article2Full._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.58: original introduction supplied by author; retain poster and title.
 if(viewArticles[0]){viewArticles[0].text="𝗚𝗥𝗘𝗘𝗧𝗜𝗡𝗚𝗦  𝗘𝗩𝗘𝗥𝗬𝗢𝗡𝗘\nᒪET ᗰE IᑎTᖇOᗪᑌᑕE ᗰYSEᒪᖴ\n\nI live together with my 14 year old foster son on a small island in southern Thailand. I used to own a small holiday resort but 2 years without tourists ruined my business. As if that wasn't enough, our home got destroyed by a falling tree during monsoon time whilst I was in hospital getting radiotherapy for my cancer on the back of my tongue. Now, three years later, I still suffer from loss of taste and eating has become a real problem. \n\nThen in 2020, whilst salvaging what was salvageable from my bungalow,  I collapsed twice and had to go to emergency where they diagnosed me with COPD (emphysema). \n\nSadly, as a foreigner living in Thailand, especially here in the deep south, health or social care is virtually non existent. We do have a small hospital but their only speciality is treating motorbike accidents. There's no pulmonary specialist around here. \n\nThe local hospital just made some x-rays and CAT scans, put me on a drip, give me oxygen and vaporis twice a day. They gave me \"Puroxan400\" and \"Mysoven600\" and after a week, they send me home. \n\nMy health insurance claims, my cancer and COPD were pre-existing conditions (absolute rubbish - never been ill in my entire life - well, I had non Hodgkin's Lymphoma back in 1980 but that is long gone) and only paid about 40% of the costs, the rest I had to pay from my savings.\n\nIn 2021, after the fifth hospital stay within six months, I came to the conclusion that what they do at the hospital, I can do at home for a fraction of the cost and bought an oxygen concentrator. I took the machine to the hospital and ask the doctors how to use and what settings I need but no one could really help me. So all I know is what I've been reading on the internet and am grateful for support groups like this one.\n\nMostly I'm pretty fine without oxygen as long I don't exert myself. I'm pretty fine for weeks without oxygen and only take my \"Puroxan 400\" twice a day. I also have an \"Acouhaler\" I should use twice but I just can't afford that, so only take it once a day. Every so often, out of the blue, I have breathing problems and need oxygen. Sometimes just a few hours, sometimes a few days. For emergencies I have some \"Ventolin\" but have only needed it three, four times during the last two years. \n\nTwo years ago, I was still pretty ok to climb up to the 3rd floor, do shopping and cooking or go out for dinner or to the beach. Now, I spend my days more or less on my porch or in my bed with aircon, watching TV or write if I'm able to concentrate. I have not seen the sea or the sunset for over a year, despite of living on a tiny island, just 1200 steps from the beach 😥 \n\nMy foster son has been taking care of me for the last three years and without him, I don't know what I would have done. He checks my medication and helps me with showers. He does the shopping, cleaning, laundry and occasional cooking (I got a cleaning lady now who comes every two weeks cleaning and doing the laundry)\n\nHe spend weeks at the time in a chair next to my hospital bed, holding my hands in desperate times. He caressed my head and massaged my pains away. He's been there for me day and night, never left my side. \n\nWhere adults failed, he carried on. Never to tired, always strong. He never complaint, not day nor night. He gives  me hope and strength when I am low and whenever I ask him how he's doing, his answer is: \" No worries Joe, I'm doing great\".\n\nCOVID lock-down, loosing my livelihood and my home, then getting cancer and COPD, having to move from my home by the sea to rented accommodation in town was just a bit too much and I got really depressed. \n\nOne doctor prescribed me antidepressants, the other CBD/THC oil. I don't really like too much chemicals if not absolute necessary. Writing about my 50 years of travelling and CBD/THC oil with the occasional joint when I feel depressed, seams to help. \n\nNow I just hope I manage to stay relative stable for the next couple of years without being too much of a burden to my foster son, at least until he's legally old enough to live by himself. \n\nI have always been an optimist and always saw a funny side on everything but COPD made it pretty difficult to be optimistic but could be worse, I could be pregnant 😜";viewArticles[0]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.59: complete author-supplied Articles 3–5, retaining their existing artwork.
 const fullTextArticle3=viewArticles[2];
 if(fullTextArticle3){fullTextArticle3.text="𝟭𝟬 𝗧𝗜𝗣𝗦 𝗧𝗢 𝗗𝗘𝗔𝗟 𝗪𝗜𝗧𝗛 𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗\nIf you have COPD, you can improve your life with good management of the disease. Always make sure you talk with your healthcare provider about questions and concerns.\n\n𝟭. 𝗚𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝘂𝗽 𝘀𝗺𝗼𝗸𝗶𝗻𝗴\nGiving up nicotine is one of the most important things you can do for your health. More than 3 million Americans quit every year. There are several helpful techniques to help you to quit. Talk with your provider to determine which one may be most helpful to you:\n\n- Medications\n- Nicotine replacement therapy\n- Self-help materials\n- Counseling\n- Group programs\n\n𝟮. 𝗘𝗮𝘁 𝗿𝗶𝗴𝗵𝘁 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗲𝘅𝗲𝗿𝗰𝗶𝘀𝗲\nShortness of breath that comes with COPD can make it hard to heat a balanced diet. Eating a healthy diet and exercising are important to keeping and improving your fitness level. Always talk with your provider before starting a diet or exercise plan and start slowly.\n\n- Eat small, more frequent well-balanced meals.\n- Use a smaller plate and portions.\n- Keep a bottle of water with you and drink\n  before you eat.\n- Eat one fresh vegetable or fruit with every\n  meal.\n- Keep moving to keep your muscle strength.\n- Use a step counter to track how much \n  you walk every day, then try to \"beat\" your\n  number by one step the next day.\n- Build muscle by lifting a can of vegetables \n  or using a exercise band.\n- If physical therapy is prescribed, do your\n  exercises and go to your appointments.\n\n𝟯. 𝗚𝗲𝘁 𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘁 \nRest is important to over all health, but there are several things that can make sleep difficult if you have COPD. Most of the sleep problems related to COPD can be helped-talk with your provider.\n\n- Try to avoid napping so you are tired at\n   bedtime.\n- Try to get 30 minutes of exercise three \n   times a week.\n- Don't do anything stimulating (exercising,\n  working, arguing) 2 hours before bedtime.\n- Have a small high-protein snack such \n  as cheese and crackers, a glass of milk \n  or handful of nuts before bed. \n  Avoid large meals and a lot of carbohydrates.\n- Keep your bedroom cool, dark, and quiet.\n- Wear socks to keep your feet warm.\n- No caffeine after 5 p.m.\n- Keep regular bedtime and wake-up times.\n\n𝟰. 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗺𝗲𝗱𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀 𝗰𝗼𝗿𝗿𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗹𝘆\nMost people with COPD take medicine to help with regular and occasional breathing problems. Your drug treatment plan is tailored to your needs you need to monitor how it is working and talk with your provider when you have questions or concerns.\n\n- Use a daily routine for taking your medicine.\n  Combine taking your drugs with another daily\n  routine such as brushing your teeth. \n  Keep your medicines with your toothbrush.\n- If you take pills a different times in the day,\n  use a medicine checklist to help you keep\n  track of when you need to take which pill.\n- Get a pillbox with sections for different \n  days of the week and even times during \n  the day to help you not miss a dose.\n- Tell your doctor about unpleasant side \n  effects they may be relieved by changing \n  the dose or medication.\n- Keep all your medicines with you while\n  traveling, never check them in your luggage.\n\n𝟱. 𝗨𝘀𝗲 𝗼𝘅𝘆𝗴𝗲𝗻 𝗮𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗽𝗿𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝘆\nSome people with COPD need oxygen therapy to help their body work properly. Oxygen therapy allows you to be more active and does not cause any harm to your lungs or body if it is used correctly. You may need it for sleep, rest and activity.\n\n𝟲. 𝗥𝗲𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗶𝗻 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗯𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗵𝗶𝗻𝗴\nLearning new breathing techniques will help you move more air in and out of your lungs. This helps decrease shortness of breath.\n\n- Diaphragmatic breathing: Breathe in slowly\n  and deeply through your nose. \n  While breathing in, push your stomach out.\n  This uses the diaphragm and the lower\n  respiratory muscles.\n- Pursed lip breathing: use the same\n  diaphragmatic breathing technique, but \n  when you breathe out, purse your lips\n  slightly like you are going to whistle. \n  Breathe out slowly through pursed lips. \n  Do not force the air out.\n  \n𝟳. 𝗔𝘃𝗼𝗶𝗱 𝗶𝗻𝗳𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀\nIf you have COPD, you have an increased risk of lung infections.\n\n- Vaccines are often recommended. \n  The influenza vaccine is recommended yearly.    \n  The pneumonia vaccine is recommended\n  every 5 to 7 years.\n- Wash your hands frequently to prevent \n  the spread of germs and infections.\n\n𝟴. 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻 𝘁𝗲𝗰𝗵𝗻𝗶𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀 𝘁𝗼 𝗯𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝘂𝗽 𝗺𝘂𝗰𝘂𝘀\nWhen mucus collects in the airways, it can make breathing difficult and can lead to infection. Your doctor can demonstrate these techniques for you. Use the techniques after using your bronchodilator medicine.\n\n- Deep coughing: take a deep breath and hold \n  it for 3 seconds. Use your stomach muscles\n  to expel the air. Avoid a hacking cough or \n  just clearing your throat.\n- Huff coughing: take a breath that is slightly\n  deeper than normal. Use your stomach\n  muscles to make a \"ha, ha, ha\" sound while\n  you exhale. Follow this by diaphragmatic\n  breathing and a deep cough if you feel \n  mucus moving.\n\n𝟵. 𝗠𝗮𝗸𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘂𝘀𝗲 𝗮𝗻 𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗽𝗹𝗮𝗻\nYour doctor or healthcare provider will help you understand when your symptoms are getting worse and what you can do about it. Do not ignore your symptoms they will not go away. Know when to call your provider and when to go to the Emergency Department.\n\n𝟭𝟬. 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻 𝗺𝗼𝗿𝗲 𝗮𝗯𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗\nYou can live a healthy and happy life with COPD. Learn about the disease. Write down your concerns or areas where you want to see change and talk with your provider.\n\nUnderstanding how the disease can impact your life and what you can do to prevent or fix problems can help you take charge of your life and live the way you want to live.";fullTextArticle3._article2FullText=true;}
 const fullTextArticle4=viewArticles[3];
 if(fullTextArticle4){fullTextArticle4.text="𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗 is an umbrella term that includes chronic bronchitis and emphysema.\nCOPD and asthma are both chronic respiratory conditions, but they have key differences.\n\n𝗖𝗔𝗨𝗦𝗘:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Often caused by long-term exposure to irritants like smoking or air pollution.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: The most common cause of emphysema is long-term smoking, but it can also result from exposure to air pollution, secondhand smoke, and certain genetic factors.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: The primary cause of chronic bronchitis is long-term exposure to irritants, typically cigarette smoke or environmental pollutants. This exposure leads to chronic inflammation and thickening of the bronchial tubes.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Usually caused by a combination of genetic and environmental factors, including allergies.\n\n𝗦𝗬𝗠𝗣𝗧𝗢𝗠𝗦;\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Symptoms include chronic cough, excessive mucus production, shortness of breath, and wheezing.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Symptoms typically include shortness of breath, coughing, wheezing, and chest tightness. These symptoms gradually worsen over time and can severely impact a person's quality of life.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Common symptoms include a persistent cough with mucus production (often lasting for at least three months in two consecutive years), shortness of breath, wheezing, chest discomfort, and fatigue.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Symptoms include recurrent wheezing, coughing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath, often triggered by allergens or irritants.\n\n𝗢𝗡𝗦𝗘𝗧:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Typically develops in adults, often after years of exposure to risk factors.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: often develops over many years of exposure to the causative factors, such as smoking. Symptoms typically appear after significant lung damage has occurred, usually in middle-aged or older individuals.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Chronic bronchitis usually develops gradually over time, often as a result of years of exposure to irritants. It's more common in individuals who smoke or have a history of smoking.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Can develop at any age, even in childhood.\n\n𝗥𝗘𝗩𝗘𝗥𝗦𝗜𝗕𝗜𝗟𝗜𝗧𝗬:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Generally, lung damage is not reversible, and airflow obstruction is persistent.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Unfortunately, emphysema is not reversible. The damage to the lung tissue is permanent and irreversible. However, further progression of the disease can be slowed or halted with appropriate treatment and lifestyle changes.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Unlike some other lung conditions, chronic bronchitis is not typically reversible. The damage to the airways and lung tissue is often permanent. However, early intervention and lifestyle changes can slow down its progression.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Often, airflow obstruction is reversible, either spontaneously or with medication.\n\n𝗧𝗥𝗘𝗔𝗧𝗠𝗘𝗡𝗧:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Treatment aims to manage symptoms, improve lung function, and prevent exacerbations. It includes bronchodilators, inhaled corticosteroids, and lifestyle changes.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Treatment aims to manage symptoms and slow the progression of emphysema. This may include quitting smoking, using bronchodilators to open airways, pulmonary rehabilitation, and oxygen therapy in severe cases. Lung transplantation is an option for some individuals.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Treatment aims to relieve symptoms and improve the patient's quality of life. This can involve lifestyle changes such as quitting smoking, avoiding irritants, and getting vaccinations (like the flu and pneumonia vaccines). Medications like bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids may be prescribed to help manage symptoms..In severe cases, oxygen therapy or pulmonary rehabilitation programs might be recommended.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Treatment focuses on controlling inflammation and preventing attacks. Medications include bronchodilators, inhaled corticosteroids, and allergen avoidance.\n\n𝗟𝗨𝗡𝗚 𝗙𝗨𝗡𝗖𝗧𝗜𝗢𝗡:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Characterized by a gradual, irreversible decline in lung function over time.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Significantly impairs lung function. It reduces the ability of the lungs to expand and contract effectively, leading to reduced oxygen exchange. Lung function tests, such as spirometry, can measure the severity of this impairment.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Impairs lung function by reducing the efficiency of air exchange in the bronchial tubes. Over time, this can lead to decreased lung capacity and difficulty breathing. Lung function can be assessed through tests like spirometry, which measures the volume and flow of air during inhalation and exhalation.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Lung function can fluctuate, and most people with asthma have normal lung function between attacks.";fullTextArticle4._article2FullText=true;}
 const fullTextArticle5=viewArticles[4];
 if(fullTextArticle5){fullTextArticle5.text="𝗢𝘅𝘆𝗴𝗲𝗻 𝗰𝗮𝗻𝗻𝘂𝗹𝗮𝘀  𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗩𝗮𝘀𝗶𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲 ♨️\n𝘊𝘢𝘯 𝘐 𝘶𝘴𝘦 𝘝𝘢𝘴𝘪𝘭𝘪𝘯𝘦 𝘵𝘰 𝘮𝘰𝘪𝘴𝘵𝘶𝘳𝘪𝘴𝘦 𝘮𝘺 𝘯𝘰𝘴𝘦 𝘸𝘩𝘪𝘭𝘴𝘵 𝘩𝘢𝘷𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘰𝘹𝘺𝘨𝘦𝘯?\n\nPetroleum jelly (Vaseline) does not simply burst into flames because someone is using oxygen. However, oxygen supports combustion, and in an oxygen-enriched environment, materials can catch fire more easily and burn much faster if exposed to a spark, cigarette or flame. That is why petroleum-based products should be kept away from oxygen equipment and the areas where oxygen is delivered. Better not to turn our noses into an unintended barbecue. 😜\n\n𝗕𝗨𝗧\nIn general, exposure to petroleum-based products, such as petroleum jelly (Vaseline) or oil-based lubricants, can lead to a deterioration of silicone over time. This can result in the cannula becoming softer, weaker, or more susceptible to damage, potentially making it less safe and effective for medical use. The extent of deterioration can vary, but it's generally advisable to avoid direct and prolonged contact between silicone cannulas and petroleum-based products.\n\n𝗙𝗮𝗰𝗶𝘁:\nIf you need to moisturise your nose whilst using oxygen, use a water based product.\n\n𝗕𝗧𝗪 ...\nThe same applies to condoms and petrol based products 😉😜";fullTextArticle5._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.61: Article 10 complete updated text, existing image preserved.
 if(viewArticles[9]){viewArticles[9].text="💨 ARE YOU USING YOU INHALER CORRECTLY\n\nOne little puff. So many ways to get it wrong. 😜\n\nHere's something worth thinking about. Studies suggest that more than half of people using inhalers make at least one mistake in their technique. And considering how much we depend on these little devices to keep us breathing, that's rather worrying.\n\nI've been using inhalers for years, and one thing I've learned is that having the right medication is only half the story. Getting it into the lungs, where it's actually supposed to do its job, is just as important. Otherwise, we're essentially giving our tongue and throat some rather expensive medication while our lungs wait patiently for their share. 😏\n\n🫁 Not all inhalers work the same way\n\nThis is probably the most important thing to understand. With a conventional pressurised inhaler, such as Ventolin, the idea is to breathe in slowly and steadily while pressing the canister. But with a dry-powder inhaler, the technique is different. You generally need a quicker, stronger inhalation to draw the medication into your lungs.\n\nAnd then there are soft-mist inhalers, breath-actuated devices and spacers. So there isn't one universal technique that works for every inhaler.\n\n😤 The little mistakes that make a big difference\n\nOne common mistake is failing to breathe out before taking a dose. Ideally, we breathe out gently, away from the mouthpiece, before inhaling the medication. No need to force every last molecule of air out of our already struggling lungs.\n\nAnother is poor coordination. With a conventional pressurised inhaler, pressing the canister too early or too late can mean much of the medication ends up in the mouth or throat rather than the lungs. That's where a spacer can be particularly helpful. It makes coordination easier and can improve medication delivery with compatible inhalers.\n\nThen there's holding our breath afterwards. Ideally, we try for up to ten seconds, or as long as comfortably possible. With severe COPD, ten seconds can sometimes feel like an eternity, so there's no point turning blue trying to impress the inhaler. 😜\n\nAnd let's not forget cleaning. Some inhalers need regular mouthpiece cleaning to prevent blockages, while others must be kept dry. Always follow the instructions for the particular device.\n\n💊 Rescue inhalers and maintenance inhalers\n\nAnother important distinction. Rescue inhalers are intended to provide relatively quick relief when symptoms suddenly worsen. Maintenance inhalers, on the other hand, are generally prescribed for regular use, even on days when we're feeling reasonably well.\n\nSkipping maintenance medication simply because we're having a good day can undermine the treatment plan. And taking more rescue medication than prescribed isn't necessarily the answer when breathing becomes difficult.\n\n🔍 A few things I always consider\n\nBefore using an inhaler, it's worth checking that it's the correct medication, that there's medication left, that the device hasn't expired and that it has been prepared according to its instructions.\n\nSome pressurised inhalers need shaking, others don't. Some require priming after a period without use. Dry-powder inhalers have their own preparation procedures. If more than one puff is prescribed, follow the recommended interval for that particular inhaler.\n\nAnd if you're using an inhaler containing corticosteroids, rinsing your mouth and spitting afterwards helps reduce the risk of oral thrush.\n\n❤️ My point?\n\nWe spend so much time worrying about oxygen levels, lung function, medication and the progression of COPD that it's surprisingly easy to overlook something as basic as how we actually use our inhalers.\n\nA simple technique check with a doctor, nurse or pharmacist can sometimes make a real difference. Ask them to watch you use your own device. Not just explain it, but actually watch. After all, there's not much point spending money on medication if half of it ends up decorating the back of your throat.\n\nThe right medication, delivered the wrong way, may not give us the benefit we're expecting, and with COPD, every little bit of effective treatment matters.\n\nTake care of those lungs, my friends. They're the only pair we've got. ❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing what I've learned from living with severe emphysema. Not medical advice. Different inhalers require different techniques, so always follow your own device instructions and prescribed treatment plan.";viewArticles[9]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.65: Article 4 text and title updated; original image remains untouched.
 if(viewArticles[3]){viewArticles[3].title="𝗢𝗕𝗦𝗧𝗥𝗨𝗖𝗧𝗜𝗩𝗘 𝘃𝘀. 𝗥𝗘𝗦𝗧𝗥𝗜𝗖𝗧𝗜𝗩𝗘 𝗜𝗠𝗣𝗔𝗜𝗥𝗠𝗘𝗡𝗧";viewArticles[3].text="Obstructive and restrictive impairments refer to two types of lung diseases that affect airflow differently.\n\n𝗢𝗯𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗜𝗺𝗽𝗮𝗶𝗿𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁:\n𝐂𝐡𝐚𝐫𝐚𝐜𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐜𝐬:\nObstructive diseases, such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and asthma, result in narrowed airways, making it difficult for air to flow out during exhalation.\n𝐄𝐟𝐟𝐞𝐜𝐭 𝐨𝐧 𝐀𝐢𝐫𝐟𝐥𝐨𝐰:\nExhalation is particularly affected, leading to increased resistance and difficulty in pushing air out of the lungs.\n𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐨𝐧 𝐅𝐞𝐚𝐭𝐮𝐫𝐞𝐬:\nSymptoms often include wheezing, shortness of breath, and a persistent cough.\n\n𝗥𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗜𝗺𝗽𝗮𝗶𝗿𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁:\n𝐂𝐡𝐚𝐫𝐚𝐜𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐜𝐬:\nRestrictive diseases, like pulmonary fibrosis and chest wall deformities, cause a reduction in lung volume and lung expansion.\n𝐄𝐟𝐟𝐞𝐜𝐭 𝐨𝐧 𝐋𝐮𝐧𝐠 𝐄𝐱𝐩𝐚𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐨𝐧:\nThe ability of the lungs to expand is compromised, limiting the amount of air the lungs can hold.\n𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐨𝐧 𝐅𝐞𝐚𝐭𝐮𝐫𝐞𝐬:\nSymptoms may include difficulty taking deep breaths, rapid breathing, and reduced lung capacity.\n\n𝗜𝗡 𝗦𝗨𝗠𝗠𝗔𝗥𝗬:\nObstructive impairment is characterized by difficulty exhaling due to narrowed airways, while restrictive impairment involves reduced lung volume and limited lung expansion during inhalation.";viewArticles[3]._article2FullText=true;}
