 // V17.69: Update Article 6 title and text only; keep its poster and all preceding articles unchanged.
 if(viewArticles[5]){viewArticles[5].title="𝗢𝘅𝘆𝗴𝗲𝗻 𝗰𝗮𝗻𝗻𝘂𝗹𝗮𝘀  𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗩𝗮𝘀𝗶𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲 ♨️";viewArticles[5].text="𝘊𝘢𝘯 𝘐 𝘶𝘴𝘦 𝘝𝘢𝘴𝘪𝘭𝘪𝘯𝘦 𝘵𝘰 𝘮𝘰𝘪𝘴𝘵𝘶𝘳𝘪𝘴𝘦 𝘮𝘺 𝘯𝘰𝘴𝘦 𝘸𝘩𝘪𝘭𝘴𝘵 𝘩𝘢𝘷𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘰𝘹𝘺𝘨𝘦𝘯?\n\nPetroleum jelly (Vaseline) does not spontaneously ignite simply because oxygen is present. However, oxygen-enriched conditions make fires start more easily and burn much more rapidly and intensely. Petroleum-based products can add to that risk, so they should not be used around oxygen equipment or inside the nose while receiving oxygen.\n\n𝗕𝗨𝗧\nThere is another consideration. In general, exposure to petroleum-based products, such as petroleum jelly (Vaseline) or oil-based lubricants, can lead to deterioration of some materials over time. Compatibility depends on the specific cannula material, so it's best to avoid direct and prolonged contact with petroleum-based products.\n\n𝗙𝗮𝗰𝗶𝘁:\nIf you need to moisturise your nose whilst using oxygen, use a water based product recommended for oxygen users.\n\n𝗕𝗧𝗪 ...\nThe same applies to condoms and petrol based products 😉😜";viewArticles[5].date='';viewArticles[5]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.70: Update only Article 7 text and title, retaining its original image and all other articles.
 if(viewArticles[6]){viewArticles[6].title="⚠️ 𝗙𝗢𝗢𝗗𝗦 𝗧𝗢 𝗪𝗔𝗧𝗖𝗛 𝗢𝗨𝗧 𝗙𝗢𝗥 𝗪𝗜𝗧𝗛 𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗";viewArticles[6].text="Living with COPD can make something as ordinary as eating surprisingly complicated. Some foods leave us feeling bloated, some are difficult to chew or swallow, and a large meal can make breathing feel like hard work. But that doesn't mean there's a universal list of foods everyone with COPD should avoid. What matters is how our own bodies respond, and making sure we still get enough nourishment.\n\n🐟 Fatty fish, egg yolks and liver\n\nFatty fish, eggs and liver provide useful nutrients, including protein and vitamins. Fatty fish also contains omega-3 fats. They're not automatically bad for COPD, and egg yolks don't necessarily put a strain on the heart simply because they contain cholesterol. However, rich or greasy meals can leave some people feeling uncomfortably full or worsen reflux, which may make breathing feel harder. If that's your experience, try smaller portions, lightly grilled or steamed fish, or eggs prepared in a way you find easier to digest. There's no need to restrict yourself to egg whites unless you have a particular reason to do so.\n\n🧀 Cheese and other dairy products\n\nCheese can be a useful source of protein and calcium, especially when keeping weight on is a struggle. Some varieties are high in salt and saturated fat, so portion size and the type of cheese may matter if you have high blood pressure, heart problems or fluid retention. Dairy doesn't generally increase mucus production, although it can leave a coating sensation in the mouth or throat that feels like thicker mucus. If cheese doesn't agree with you, try smaller amounts or lower-sodium varieties. There's no need to replace it with vitamin K2 supplements simply because you have COPD.\n\n🥦 Broccoli, peppers, beans and whole grains\n\nVegetables, legumes and whole grains offer fibre, vitamins and other valuable nutrients. The problem for some of us is gas and bloating, especially after large servings of beans or cruciferous vegetables. When the abdomen feels swollen, the diaphragm has less room to move comfortably, and breathlessness may feel worse. Smaller servings, well-cooked vegetables, or gradually introducing fibre can help. If broccoli or beans leave you bloated, carrots, courgettes, peppers and other vegetables you tolerate may be easier alternatives. Smoothies or soups can also be useful when chewing or eating is tiring.\n\n🥜 Nuts and seeds\n\nNuts and seeds provide energy, healthy fats and nutrients, which can be particularly helpful for people who struggle to maintain their weight. But whole nuts can be difficult to chew and may be a choking hazard if swallowing is impaired. Smooth nut butters may be easier for some people, although sticky foods can also be troublesome for others with swallowing difficulties. Ground nuts, suitable spreads or soft avocado are alternatives, depending on what you can swallow safely. If choking is a concern, ask a swallowing specialist about appropriate textures.\n\n🥕 Carrots, sweet potatoes and leafy greens\n\nThese are nutritious foods, not foods to avoid. Raw carrots or tough leafy greens may simply be difficult to chew, particularly without teeth or with swallowing problems. Steaming, mashing, puréeing or blending them into soup can make eating easier. Sweet potatoes are a perfectly reasonable food too; as with anything else, a very large meal may leave you feeling too full to breathe comfortably.\n\n✅ What works better for me to keep in mind\n\nRather than banning whole food groups, I'd concentrate on smaller, more frequent meals, foods that are easy to chew and swallow, and enough protein and calories to maintain strength. Soft vegetables, soups, smoothies, eggs, fish, yoghurt, avocado and other foods you tolerate can all have a place. Fizzy drinks can make bloating worse for some people, while staying adequately hydrated can help with general wellbeing and mucus clearance, unless your doctor has advised fluid restrictions.\n\nVitamin supplements aren't automatically better than food and they don't replace a balanced diet. Some can interact with medicines or be harmful in excessive doses. If you're losing weight, having trouble swallowing or considering supplements, a doctor or dietitian can help you work out what you actually need.\n\n❤️ My point?\n\nWith COPD, eating should help us keep our strength, not become another list of things we're frightened to do. Listen to your body, adapt the portions and textures, and don't give up nutritious foods simply because somebody has put them on an internet 'avoid' list. 😜❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing practical information about living with COPD, not medical advice. Individual dietary needs vary, particularly if you have heart disease, diabetes, swallowing difficulties or other medical conditions.";viewArticles[6]._article2FullText=true;}
 // V17.71: Update only Article 8 title and text; preserve its existing picture and all other articles.
 if(viewArticles[7]){viewArticles[7].title="🫁 WAYS TO OPEN THE BRONCHIAL TUBES";viewArticles[7].text="The bronchial tubes are the airways that carry air in and out of our lungs. With COPD, these airways can become narrowed, inflamed or clogged with mucus, making breathing considerably harder. Over the years I've learned there are quite a few things that can help us breathe a little easier, but there's an important difference between actually opening the airways and simply making better use of the lungs we've got.\n\n💊 1. BRONCHODILATOR MEDICATIONS\n\nThese are the main medications that actually relax the muscles around our airways, allowing them to widen so air can move more freely. Short-acting bronchodilators, such as salbutamol (Ventolin or albuterol), usually begin working within minutes and can last around four to six hours. Ipratropium (Atrovent) works differently and generally takes a little longer to kick in. They're often prescribed to relieve symptoms when breathing becomes more difficult.\n\nThen there are the long-acting bronchodilators used for regular maintenance. LABAs include formoterol and salmeterol, while LAMAs include tiotropium and umeclidinium. Combination inhalers such as Anoro and Spiolto contain two long-acting bronchodilators, while Trelegy also includes an inhaled corticosteroid. The important thing is knowing which inhaler is for regular maintenance and which is prescribed for quick relief. Not every inhaler is meant to rescue us when we're suddenly struggling.\n\n🌬️ 2. NEBULIZED THERAPY\n\nA nebulizer turns liquid medication into a fine mist that we breathe through a mouthpiece or mask. Depending on what's prescribed, it might contain salbutamol, ipratropium or a combination of both. It can be useful if using an ordinary inhaler is difficult, although a nebulizer isn't automatically more effective than an inhaler used correctly with a spacer.\n\nAnd a little distinction worth remembering: saline may help moisten the airways or loosen secretions in some circumstances, but it doesn't actually open the bronchial tubes. Only use solutions and medications suitable for your nebulizer and recommended by your healthcare team.\n\n🧘 3. BREATHING TECHNIQUES\n\nThese don't directly relax the muscles around our bronchial tubes, but they can help us cope with breathlessness and reduce air trapping. Pursed-lip breathing, or PLB, has become one of my most useful little tools for living with severe emphysema. I breathe in gently through my nose, then breathe out slowly through pursed lips, almost as if I'm blowing out a candle. That little bit of back-pressure helps keep the smaller airways open longer while breathing out.\n\nDiaphragmatic or belly-focused breathing can help some people breathe more efficiently too, although with severe COPD it doesn't always feel comfortable or useful. I've learned not to force a technique simply because somebody says it's good for everyone. Find what works for your own lungs.\n\n💧 4. HYDRATION AND MUCUS CLEARANCE\n\nThick mucus can make already narrowed airways even more troublesome. Drinking enough fluids, unless you've been told to restrict them, can help keep you adequately hydrated. Gentle huff-coughing may help move mucus, and some people benefit from airway-clearance physiotherapy or a suitable PEP device recommended by their respiratory team.\n\nHumidification can sometimes help with comfort, but breathing hot steam has little proven benefit and can cause nasty burns. Too much humidity can encourage mould as well, which is hardly what our lungs need. And drinking gallons of water doesn't automatically make mucus thinner. As usual, more isn't necessarily better. 😜\n\n🚫 5. AVOID AIRWAY TRIGGERS\n\nSometimes making breathing easier is less about adding something and more about avoiding what makes it worse. Cigarette smoke, secondhand smoke, dust, pollution, strong chemical fumes, aerosols, perfumes, incense, cold air and respiratory infections can all aggravate our breathing. But we're not all affected in exactly the same way. What sends one person's lungs into a protest might hardly bother somebody else. Learning to recognise our own triggers can make everyday life considerably easier.\n\n🌿 6. NATURAL AND SUPPORTIVE MEASURES\n\nA warm drink, ginger tea or another soothing beverage may make our throat feel more comfortable, and there's nothing wrong with enjoying that. But feeling soothed isn't the same as opening narrowed bronchial tubes. There's no convincing evidence that mullein, peppermint steam or other herbal remedies reliably work as bronchodilators for COPD. Some herbs can interact with medication, while strong scents and inhaled oils can actually irritate sensitive airways.\n\nSo by all means enjoy your cup of tea if it helps you feel better. Just don't expect it to do the job of your prescribed inhaler. 😉\n\n💜 MY FINAL THOUGHT\n\nLiving with COPD has taught me that there isn't one magical solution. Medication can help open the airways, breathing techniques help us use what we've got more effectively, and mucus clearance, exercise, avoiding triggers and getting to know our own bodies all play their part.\n\nPerhaps most importantly, we need to recognise the difference between our usual breathlessness and something that isn't normal for us. Sudden or severe breathing difficulty, chest pain, confusion or blue lips need urgent medical attention, not another cup of herbal tea.\n\nOpening the bronchial tubes won't repair damaged alveoli, but it may help us make better use of the lungs we've still got. And with severe emphysema, I've learned to appreciate every little bit of easier breathing.\n\nStay safe and breathe as easily as possible. 💜❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing what I've learned from living with severe emphysema and general COPD information. Not medical advice. Always follow your own prescribed treatment plan.";viewArticles[7]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.72: Article 11 text and title only.
 if(viewArticles[10]){viewArticles[10].title="😤 UNDERSTANDING OXYGEN THERAPY";viewArticles[10].text="Nasal cannulas, reservoir cannulas and oxygen masks — what's the difference?\n\nWhen we're living with COPD, oxygen equipment can become part of everyday life. But a standard nasal cannula, an Oxymizer reservoir cannula and an oxygen mask aren't simply three versions of the same thing. They deliver oxygen differently, and choosing between them isn't just a matter of turning up the flow. Here's how I understand it after years of living with severe emphysema.\n\n🌬️ 1. STANDARD NASAL CANNULA\n\nThis is the familiar little tube with two prongs sitting just inside our nostrils. It lets us talk, eat and drink while receiving supplemental oxygen, which makes it practical for everyday use. Standard cannulas are commonly used with continuous oxygen flows of around 1–6 litres per minute, although the appropriate flow depends on the person and the equipment.\n\nYou'll sometimes see figures suggesting that each extra litre adds a certain percentage of oxygen. Those are only rough estimates. The actual oxygen concentration we breathe depends on how fast and deeply we're breathing, whether we breathe through our mouth, and other factors. A flow setting isn't a guaranteed FiO₂. And the number on a portable pulse-dose concentrator isn't necessarily the same as litres per minute of continuous flow.\n\n💨 2. RESERVOIR CANNULA — THE OXYMIZER\n\nAn Oxymizer looks rather like a nasal cannula with a small reservoir built into it. It stores some oxygen during exhalation so more is available at the beginning of the next breath. This can help conserve oxygen or deliver a comparable effect at a lower flow than an ordinary cannula in some circumstances.\n\nBut it isn't simply a 'higher oxygen' cannula, and there's no universal flow or oxygen percentage that applies to everyone. The appropriate setting depends on the particular device, the prescription and how well it works for the person using it. As with other nasal oxygen devices, dryness or irritation can be a nuisance, and any humidification needs to be compatible with the equipment and recommended by the oxygen provider.\n\n😷 3. OXYGEN MASKS — NOT ALL THE SAME\n\nHere's where things can get confusing. A simple oxygen face mask, a Venturi mask and a non-rebreather mask with a reservoir bag are different devices. A simple mask generally needs a minimum flow of about 5 litres per minute to avoid rebreathing exhaled carbon dioxide, and it provides a variable oxygen concentration. A Venturi mask is designed to deliver a more controlled oxygen concentration, which can be especially useful when precise oxygen delivery matters. A non-rebreather reservoir mask can deliver a high oxygen concentration in emergencies when used correctly with an adequate flow.\n\nA mask covers the nose and mouth, which isn't very convenient when we're trying to eat or have a conversation. More importantly, a mask isn't automatically the right answer simply because somebody feels very breathless. Severe breathing difficulty needs urgent medical assessment, not an experiment with different oxygen devices at home.\n\n⚠️ THE IMPORTANT PART — MORE OXYGEN ISN'T ALWAYS BETTER\n\nI've learned that the aim of oxygen therapy is to correct low oxygen levels, not to chase the highest number on the oximeter. Some people with COPD are susceptible to carbon dioxide retention, and excessive oxygen can make that worse. During an acute exacerbation, clinicians often initially target an oxygen saturation of 88–92% in people at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure, pending blood-gas results. But individual targets and home prescriptions can differ, so follow your own medical team's instructions rather than changing flows based on a general rule from the internet.\n\nAnd remember, a pulse oximeter tells us about oxygen saturation, not how much carbon dioxide we're retaining. Someone can have a reassuring-looking SpO₂ and still be unwell.\n\n❤️ MY POINT?\n\nA standard cannula is often comfortable for routine oxygen use. A reservoir cannula may help make better use of the available oxygen. Different masks have different purposes, including carefully controlled oxygen delivery and emergency treatment. None of them is automatically 'best' for every person with COPD.\n\nMake sure the device, flow setting and oxygen target are the ones prescribed for you. Check the fit and keep the equipment clean as instructed. If you're suddenly much more breathless than usual, confused, unusually drowsy, having chest pain or turning blue around the lips, seek urgent medical help. That's not the moment to play around with the oxygen knob. 😜\n\nAs I always say, we're individuals, not statistics. Learn what your own lungs need, and use oxygen as a treatment, not as a competition to see who can reach 100%. ❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing what I've learned from living with severe emphysema and general COPD information. Not medical advice. Follow your own oxygen prescription and treatment plan.";viewArticles[10]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.73: Update only Article 12; keep all prior articles, images, and ordering intact.
 if(viewArticles[11]){viewArticles[11].title="𝗛𝗢𝗪 𝗔𝗖𝗖𝗨𝗥𝗔𝗧𝗘 𝗔𝗥𝗘 𝗢𝗫𝗜𝗠𝗘𝗧𝗘𝗥𝗦?";viewArticles[11].date="";viewArticles[11].text="Pulse oximeters are useful little devices, especially for those of us living with COPD. But there's something worth remembering before we start worrying about every number on the screen: they're not quite as precise as many people think. 😜\n\n📊 HOW ACCURATE ARE THEY?\n\nWhen used correctly, a good, properly validated fingertip oximeter can give a reasonably accurate estimate of our blood oxygen saturation. In the 90–100% SpO₂ range, many medical-grade devices have a stated accuracy of around ±2–3 percentage points compared with arterial blood measurements. But that figure describes performance across many measurements, not a promise that every single reading is within two or three points of the truth.\n\nSo if the screen says 94%, don't automatically assume your actual oxygen saturation is exactly 94%. It may be higher or lower. And as oxygen levels fall, especially below 90% and more so below 80%, readings generally become less dependable. A blood-gas test may be needed when doctors require a more reliable assessment, particularly if someone is unwell.\n\n✅ WHAT HELPS GET A BETTER READING?\n\nWarm hands, good circulation, a correctly positioned sensor and keeping your hand still all help. The index or middle finger is commonly used, although the best finger can depend on the device. Give the reading a little time to settle rather than trusting the first number that flashes up.\n\n⚠️ WHAT CAN FOOL THE DEVICE?\n\nCold fingers, poor circulation, movement, tremors, dark nail polish, artificial nails and strong external light can all interfere with readings. Skin pigmentation matters too: research has found that some pulse oximeters can overestimate oxygen saturation in people with darker skin, potentially missing dangerously low levels. Even an apparently normal reading doesn't rule out a problem if you feel seriously unwell.\n\n📌 THE IMPORTANT BIT — WATCH THE TREND\n\nPersonally, I find an oximeter most useful for noticing changes rather than treating one reading as an absolute verdict on how I'm doing. Is my oxygen level unusually low for me? Is it falling? Does the reading make sense alongside how I actually feel? Those questions are often more useful than panicking over a single digit.\n\nThere's no one magic number that applies to everybody with COPD. Some people have individual oxygen targets, and others may normally run lower than the general population. A reading that's unexpectedly low for you, a persistent downward trend, or symptoms that don't match the number deserve medical attention. Severe or sudden breathlessness, chest pain, confusion or blue lips need urgent assessment regardless of what the oximeter says.\n\n👉 MY RECOMMENDATION\n\nIf your budget allows, I like the idea of an oximeter that can save readings or connect by Bluetooth. Not because Bluetooth makes it more accurate — it doesn't — but because a record over time can show trends far better than a quick check followed by unnecessary panic. Just make sure the device itself is reliable and learn how to use it properly.\n\nAs I've said before, numbers are useful tools, but they don't get to decide how we feel. We're individuals, not statistics. ❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing what I've learned from living with severe emphysema. Not medical advice. Follow your own prescribed oxygen targets and seek medical help when symptoms or readings are concerning.";viewArticles[11]._article2FullText=true;}
 // V17.74: remove displayed Article 9; all subsequent articles renumber automatically.
 viewArticles.splice(8,1);
 if(viewArticles[11]){viewArticles[11].title="Breathing Techniques";viewArticles[11]._subtitle="When Your Lungs Refuse To Cooperate";viewArticles[11].text="Living with COPD, one breath at a time.\n\nOne of the strangest things about living with COPD is that something we've been doing automatically since the day we were born suddenly becomes something we have to think about. Breathing! Who would have thought that one day I'd have to learn how to breathe properly? After all, I've been practising for almost 70 years. You'd think I'd have got the hang of it by now. 😜\n\nBut with severe emphysema, breathing isn't always as straightforward as taking air in and letting it out again. Sometimes getting the air out is actually the bigger problem. Over the years, I've learned a few techniques that help me manage my breathing, particularly when I'm struggling. They don't repair damaged lungs, and what works for me won't necessarily work for everyone, but they've certainly made some difficult moments more manageable.\n\n🫁 Pursed-lip breathing — my old faithful\n\nOf all the breathing techniques I've tried, pursed-lip breathing is probably the one I rely on most. The idea is simple. I breathe in gently through my nose, then slowly breathe out through pursed lips, almost as if I'm blowing out a candle without wanting to extinguish it. I generally aim to make the exhalation longer than the inhalation. Two seconds in and four seconds out is a useful starting point, but I don't sit there with a stopwatch. My lungs have enough complaints without me giving them deadlines. 😏\n\nThe gentle resistance created by pursing the lips helps keep the smaller airways open a little longer, allowing more trapped air to escape. And that's particularly important with emphysema, where damaged airways can collapse during exhalation. It doesn't magically fill my lungs with oxygen, but it can reduce air trapping and help me regain control when breathing becomes uncomfortable.\n\n🌬️ Belly breathing — sounds easier than it is\n\nAnother technique is diaphragmatic breathing, sometimes called belly breathing. The idea is to breathe gently using the diaphragm rather than relying excessively on the muscles around the upper chest and shoulders. You can try placing one hand on your chest and the other on your belly, breathing in gently and allowing the belly to rise, then slowly breathing out again. Sounds simple enough, doesn't it?\n\nUnfortunately, with advanced emphysema, the diaphragm can become flattened by overinflated lungs, making this technique surprisingly difficult. Some people find it helpful. Others, particularly those with severe COPD, actually feel more breathless trying it. So I wouldn't force it. Breathing exercises are supposed to make life easier, not turn into another Olympic event.\n\n🚶 Breathing and movement — timing matters\n\nOne thing I've learned is that how we breathe during physical activity can make quite a difference. Take standing up from a chair, for example. Rather than holding my breath while pushing myself upright, I try to breathe in beforehand and breathe out during the effort. The same principle applies to bending, lifting, walking or doing household jobs. It's sometimes called 'blow as you go', and I rather like that description. Of course, remembering to coordinate breathing while concentrating on not falling flat on my face is another matter entirely. 😂 But it helps prevent breath-holding during exertion and can make certain activities feel less exhausting.\n\n🪑 Sometimes the best technique is simply sitting properly\n\nWhen I become breathless, one of the first things I do is find a comfortable position. Leaning slightly forward, with my elbows resting on my knees or my arms supported on a table, often makes breathing easier. This is commonly called the tripod position. Supporting the arms and upper body can help the accessory breathing muscles work more effectively. Some people find standing and leaning forward against a counter helpful too. At night, raising the upper body with pillows or an adjustable bed can sometimes make breathing more comfortable.\n\nPersonally, I've spent enough nights sitting up wondering whether my lungs have forgotten what they're supposed to do. But regularly waking up gasping isn't something we should simply accept as normal COPD. It can have several causes and deserves medical attention.\n\n🌴 A little fresh air and a fan can work wonders\n\nLiving in Thailand, I have plenty of experience with heat and humidity. And believe me, struggling to breathe while sweating like a pig isn't my idea of a relaxing tropical holiday. I've found that a small fan blowing gently towards my face can be surprisingly comforting. There's actually research supporting this. Airflow across the face can reduce the sensation of breathlessness in some people, even without changing the oxygen level in the blood.\n\nIt doesn't replace medication or oxygen when needed, but it's a simple little trick that costs next to nothing. I also try to avoid smoke, dust, strong perfumes and anything else that seems determined to annoy my lungs. After more than fifty years of smoking, I suppose they've earned the right to complain. 😜\n\n💧 Mucus, hydration and keeping things moving\n\nFor those of us who struggle with mucus, staying adequately hydrated can help keep secretions from becoming unnecessarily thick. That doesn't mean drinking gallons of water. Some people have medical reasons to limit fluids, and more isn't automatically better. Gentle airway-clearance techniques, including huff coughing, can also help move mucus without the exhaustion of repeated violent coughing.\n\nAnd then there's exercise. I know, I know. When you can barely breathe, being told to exercise sounds like somebody's idea of a cruel joke. But appropriately paced activity, whether walking, swimming, cycling or doing seated exercises, can strengthen muscles and improve endurance. It won't regenerate destroyed alveoli, but it can help us make better use of the capacity we've still got. The important thing is to work within our individual limits, preferably with guidance from a pulmonary rehabilitation professional, rather than trying to prove something to ourselves.\n\n💊 Don't forget the medical side\n\nBreathing techniques are useful tools, but they're not replacements for prescribed treatment. Bronchodilator inhalers can help relax the muscles around the airways, and using the correct inhaler technique is important. It's worth having a doctor or pharmacist check occasionally because even experienced patients can develop bad habits.\n\nFor those prescribed oxygen, the flow or setting should follow an individual treatment plan. Breathlessness doesn't always mean low oxygen, and more oxygen isn't necessarily better, particularly for people at risk of retaining carbon dioxide. And if breathing suddenly becomes much worse, doesn't respond to the prescribed rescue plan, or comes with chest pain, confusion, fainting or blue lips, that's a reason to seek emergency medical help, not to keep practising breathing exercises.\n\n🧠 And then there's the brain...\n\nThis is probably one of the most difficult parts of COPD. When breathing becomes difficult, the brain can interpret it as danger. Anxiety increases, muscles tighten, breathing becomes less controlled, and before we know it, we're caught in that miserable circle of breathlessness and panic. I've been there plenty of times. Telling myself not to panic doesn't always work. In fact, sometimes it seems to remind my brain that panicking is an available option!\n\nWhat helps me is recognising what's happening, getting into a comfortable position and concentrating on a gentle, unforced exhalation. Sometimes I use pursed-lip breathing. Sometimes I simply sit quietly, listen to music or watch the world go by. And sometimes I sit outside under my favourite tree, enjoying the night air while my mind wanders off in completely unnecessary directions. But I never assume that new or unusual breathlessness is just anxiety. With COPD, there's always a reason to take significant changes seriously.\n\n❤️ So what have I learned?\n\nAfter all these years, I've come to realise that managing COPD isn't about finding one magical breathing technique. It's about collecting little tools and learning when to use them. A different position, a slower exhalation, a fan, a little movement, a rest before becoming exhausted, or simply knowing when to ask for help. None of these things will give me back the lungs I had twenty years ago. But they can help me get through today. And that's really what matters to me.\n\nI don't need to win a breathing competition. I just want enough breath left to enjoy my cappuccino, annoy a few friends and continue zooming around Koh Phangan in my wheelchair.\n\nI manage, I don't fight.\n\nOne breath at a time, my friends. ❤️🤗\n\nJust my personal experiences and some knowledge I've picked up along the way. Not medical advice. Always discuss new breathing exercises, oxygen adjustments or significant changes in symptoms with your healthcare professional.";}

 // V17.77: Article 13 text only; preserve all preceding articles and images.
 if(viewArticles[12]){viewArticles[12].title="Pulmonary Rehabilitation Routine for COPD";viewArticles[12].text="Pulmonary rehabilitation isn't about repairing damaged lungs. If only it were that easy! 😜 It's about learning how to make better use of the lungs we've got, building strength and confidence, and making everyday life a little less exhausting. And there's no single routine that suits everyone. Someone with mild COPD may manage a good walk, while someone with severe emphysema might begin with a few minutes of seated movement. Both count.\n\n🫁 1. BREATHING TECHNIQUES\n\nPursed-lip breathing is one of the most useful techniques I've learned. Breathe in gently through the nose and breathe out slowly through pursed lips, ideally a little longer than you breathed in. Two seconds in and four out can be a starting point, but there's no need to turn it into a timed competition. Practise when comfortable so it's easier to remember when you're breathless.\n\nDiaphragmatic, or belly, breathing can help some people, but with advanced emphysema our overinflated lungs can make it uncomfortable or even increase breathlessness. Don't force it. And during activity, remember to breathe out during the effort — standing, bending or lifting — rather than holding your breath. 'Blow as you go' is a rather good way of remembering it.\n\n🚶 2. EXERCISE — A LITTLE, REGULARLY\n\nExercise sounds like a cruel joke when you're already struggling for breath, doesn't it? But appropriately paced movement can strengthen the muscles we use every day and make ordinary activities easier. Start with a gentle warm-up, then try walking, a stationary bicycle, swimming or water aerobics if they're comfortable and safe for you. Seated exercises are just as legitimate when walking isn't an option. Personally, I've found ways to keep moving even from my wheelchair.\n\nThe often-quoted target of twenty or thirty minutes isn't a starting requirement. Some people begin with just a minute or two, rest, and repeat. Light resistance bands or weights can help maintain strength, while gentle stretching and balance exercises have their place too. Finish with a cool-down. A pulmonary rehabilitation professional can help tailor the intensity, especially if you have severe COPD, heart problems, falls risk or use oxygen. And swimming isn't for everybody — water pressure and exertion can make breathing harder for some.\n\n💨 3. MUCUS AND AIRWAY CLEARANCE\n\nIf mucus is a problem, gentle huff coughing may help move it without endless exhausting coughing fits. Think of breathing out with an open mouth as if you're trying to mist up a mirror. Some people benefit from airway-clearance devices or positioning techniques, but postural drainage isn't suitable for everyone, so it's worth getting individual guidance. Staying adequately hydrated helps prevent dehydration, although drinking more than your body needs won't magically wash the mucus away. Follow any fluid restriction your doctor has given you.\n\n🥣 4. FOOD, WEIGHT AND STRENGTH\n\nNutrition matters because breathing itself takes energy, and losing muscle makes everything harder. I learned that lesson rather dramatically after my long hospital stay. Small, frequent meals can be easier than one enormous plate that leaves you feeling like you've swallowed a football. Protein-rich foods such as eggs, fish, chicken, beans or suitable nutritional drinks can help support muscle maintenance, depending on what you can comfortably chew and swallow.\n\nIf certain foods cause bloating, adjust them to your own tolerance rather than avoiding entire healthy food groups. Being underweight or carrying excess weight can both complicate breathing. Supplements such as vitamin D, calcium or B12 aren't automatically necessary for everyone with COPD; they're best chosen when there's a medical or nutritional reason, with professional advice.\n\n🧠 5. THE MIND NEEDS REHABILITATION TOO\n\nCOPD doesn't only affect the lungs. Breathlessness can make us anxious, and anxiety can make breathlessness feel worse. I've certainly had my share of those moments. Relaxation, music, gentle mindfulness, support groups and practical strategies for managing panic can help. So can a sensible sleep routine. Raising the upper body may make nights more comfortable for some people, but repeatedly waking up gasping deserves medical attention rather than being dismissed as 'just COPD'.\n\n🚭 6. EVERYDAY ADJUSTMENTS\n\nIf you smoke, stopping is one of the most important steps you can take to slow further damage. Keep recommended vaccinations up to date, including influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19 and RSV where appropriate for your age and medical circumstances. Avoid smoke, dust, fumes, mould and whatever else reliably sets your lungs complaining.\n\nIf oxygen has been prescribed, use it according to your individual plan — don't assume more is better, or change the flow simply because you're breathless. Saving energy matters too. Sit down for tasks when possible, keep things within reach, break jobs into smaller steps and rest before becoming completely exhausted. That's not giving in. That's being sensible.\n\n📅 A REALISTIC WEEKLY ROUTINE\n\nA typical day might begin with a few minutes of comfortable breathing practice and gentle stretching. Later, do whatever aerobic activity is safe for you — perhaps a short walk, a few minutes on a stationary bike or some seated exercises — with rests whenever needed. Add gentle mucus-clearance techniques if you need them, and finish the day with something relaxing rather than another battle with your lungs.\n\nOn two or three days a week, include suitable strength exercises if your rehabilitation team agrees. Keep a simple record of symptoms, activity tolerance and any oxygen readings you've been advised to monitor. Checking your weight from time to time can also reveal unwanted changes. This is a flexible example, not a prescription or a test you have to pass.\n\n🚨 WHEN NOT TO PUSH THROUGH\n\nSudden or severe breathlessness at rest, chest pain, confusion, fainting or blue lips need urgent medical attention. An oxygen reading significantly below your prescribed target, especially if it stays low or comes with symptoms, also needs prompt action according to your care plan. New leg swelling, unexpected weight changes or a worsening cough with changes in sputum deserve medical review. Green or brown mucus alone doesn't prove a bacterial infection, but a change from your usual symptoms is worth taking seriously.\n\n❤️ MY POINT?\n\nThe goal of pulmonary rehabilitation isn't to cure COPD or grow new alveoli. It's to build whatever strength and endurance we can, manage breathlessness more effectively and hold on to as much independence and enjoyment as possible. Some days we'll manage more than others. That's life with COPD.\n\nI don't need to break any records. If I can get out, enjoy my cappuccino, have a chat and zoom around Koh Phangan in my wheelchair, that's a pretty good day in my book. 😜❤️🤗\n\nI manage, I don't fight.\n\nJust sharing personal experience and general information, not a medical exercise prescription. Please work with your healthcare professional or pulmonary rehabilitation team to find a safe routine for your own condition.";viewArticles[12]._article2FullText=true;}

 if(viewArticles[13]){viewArticles[13].title="My Emphysema Rescue Flowchart";viewArticles[13].text="Not medical advice — just how I deal with it.\n\nLiving with severe emphysema has taught me that when my breathing suddenly becomes difficult, the most important thing is to recognise what's happening and know what to do next. I've developed my own little routine over the years, but it isn't a universal rescue plan. What works for me may not be suitable for someone else, and emergency symptoms are never something to sit around experimenting with. 😜\n\n🚨 FIRST — RECOGNISE WHEN IT'S AN EMERGENCY\n\nA sudden increase in breathlessness, wheezing, chest tightness or coughing can be a sign that something is wrong. But severe breathlessness at rest, blue or grey lips, confusion, fainting, or chest pain are warning signs that need emergency medical help immediately. Don't work your way through the rest of this routine first. Call for help, and follow the emergency instructions you've been given.\n\n🪑 STEP 1 — GET COMFORTABLE\n\nIf I'm breathless but not experiencing those emergency warning signs, I stop what I'm doing and sit upright, often leaning slightly forward with my arms supported. Trying to push on through an episode usually achieves very little except making my lungs even more annoyed with me. I give myself room to breathe and avoid unnecessary movement.\n\n💊 STEP 2 — FOLLOW MY PRESCRIBED RESCUE PLAN\n\nI have used medicines such as Ventolin (salbutamol) and Meptin (procaterol) as part of my own treatment over the years. But I wouldn't tell another person how many puffs or tablets to take, or when to repeat them. Different medications, inhalers and medical conditions require different instructions. The important thing is to follow your own doctor's written rescue plan, use the inhaler correctly, and know when that plan says to seek urgent help. If symptoms are worsening or not responding as expected, don't keep repeating medication indefinitely.\n\n🌬️ STEP 3 — SLOW THE EXHALATION\n\nPursed-lip breathing is one of my most useful little tools. I breathe in gently through my nose, then breathe out slowly through pursed lips, making the exhalation a little longer than the inhalation. Two seconds in and four seconds out can be a starting point, but there's no prize for keeping perfect time. 😏 I don't force a deep breath or hold my breath when I'm already struggling. The idea is to reduce air trapping and regain some control, not to turn breathing into an Olympic event.\n\n🫁 STEP 4 — CHECK MY OXYGEN, IF PRESCRIBED\n\nIf I'm using oxygen, I check that the equipment is working, the tubing isn't kinked and the cannula is properly positioned. I use the setting prescribed for me, or the adjustment instructions specifically agreed with my healthcare team. Breathlessness doesn't always mean my oxygen level is low, and turning the oxygen up without a plan isn't automatically safer — especially for people with COPD who can retain carbon dioxide.\n\n🔎 STEP 5 — SEE WHETHER I'M IMPROVING\n\nI pay attention to how I feel, whether I can speak comfortably, and whether my breathing is settling. My oximeter can help show a trend, but I don't let one number overrule serious symptoms. If I improve, I rest and take things gently. If I don't improve, become worse, or develop any emergency warning signs, it's time to get medical help. No heroic stubbornness required.\n\n❤️ STEP 6 — AFTER THE EPISODE\n\nOnce things have settled, I make a note of what happened, what I was doing beforehand, which rescue medication I used and whether I needed oxygen. A severe, unusual or repeated episode deserves a conversation with a healthcare professional, even if I eventually feel better. Those details can help identify triggers and decide whether my treatment plan needs reviewing.\n\nMY POINT?\n\nOver the years I've learned to manage my emphysema rather than fight every breath. Having a simple plan helps me stay calmer, but knowing when to stop managing things myself and ask for help is just as important.\n\nI manage, I don't fight. And I'd rather be back enjoying my cappuccino than trying to prove how stubborn I can be. 😜❤️🤗\n\nThis is my personal experience, not medical advice or a substitute for an individual COPD action plan. Follow your own prescribed rescue medication and oxygen instructions. Severe or rapidly worsening symptoms need emergency medical attention.";viewArticles[13]._article2FullText=true;}

 if(viewArticles[14]){viewArticles[14].title="Herbs, Teas & COPD";viewArticles[14].text="What may help, what remains unproven, and what deserves caution.\n\nLiving with COPD, most of us have probably tried a herbal tea or two, hoping to make breathing a little easier. And why not? A warm drink can be comforting, particularly when coughing has left our throats feeling like sandpaper. But there's a difference between something that makes us feel better and something that actually opens our bronchial tubes. No herb or tea has been reliably shown to reverse emphysema, regenerate damaged alveoli or replace prescribed COPD medication. That doesn't mean we can't enjoy them. It simply means knowing what we're drinking and what we can realistically expect.\n\n🍵 HERBAL TEAS & INFUSIONS\n\nMullein leaf has traditionally been used for coughs and respiratory discomfort, although reliable clinical evidence of benefit in COPD is lacking. Chamomile may help us relax and soothe an irritated throat, but anyone allergic to related plants should be careful, and it can interact with medicines such as warfarin. Peppermint gives that lovely cooling sensation that can make breathing feel easier without necessarily improving airflow; it can also aggravate acid reflux.\n\nThyme is another traditional remedy for coughs and mucus. Some preparations have limited evidence for ordinary coughs, but not established benefits for COPD. Turmeric contains curcumin, which shows anti-inflammatory activity in laboratory studies, but whether a cup of turmeric tea actually helps COPD remains unproven. Concentrated turmeric supplements can have interactions and, in some cases, cause liver injury. Lemongrass makes a pleasant aromatic tea, but there's no convincing evidence it improves lung function.\n\nGinseng has been studied for fatigue and respiratory symptoms, with inconsistent results, and may affect blood sugar, sleep or other medication. Holy basil, or tulsi, is traditionally used for stress and general well-being, but we don't have sufficient evidence of a COPD benefit. Nettle leaf contains nutrients but hasn't been shown to improve COPD and may interact with medicines. Plantain leaf has traditionally been used to soothe irritated mucous membranes, although COPD-specific evidence is limited.\n\nMarshmallow root and slippery elm contain soothing mucilage that may help an irritated throat, but they can also interfere with the absorption of oral medicines. Fennel seed is commonly used for digestion and sometimes for coughs, but hasn't been shown to open narrowed bronchial tubes. Most ordinary, food-strength herbal teas are tolerated by many adults. Still, allergies, medication interactions and individual medical conditions matter. Natural doesn't automatically mean harmless.\n\n🌬️ STEAM & HERBAL INHALATION\n\nHere's where I'd be particularly careful. Steam inhalation is often recommended as a natural way to loosen mucus, but there's little good evidence that it improves breathing in COPD. Hot steam can cause serious burns, while strong fragrances and essential oils can irritate sensitive airways, trigger coughing or even cause bronchospasm.\n\nEucalyptus and peppermint may give a sensation of clearer breathing without actually widening the airways. Thyme, chamomile, sage, rosemary and oregano are familiar kitchen herbs, but inhaling their concentrated oils has no established COPD benefit. Hyssop essential oil is especially worth avoiding because it can be toxic and may provoke seizures.\n\nPersonally, I'd rather enjoy a comfortably warm drink than stick my face over a bowl of boiling water and hope for a miracle. 😜 And please, never put herbal oils, homemade infusions or essential oils into a medical nebulizer.\n\n🍲 HERBS & SPICES IN FOOD\n\nThis is probably the simplest way to enjoy herbs without turning the kitchen into an experimental pharmacy. Garlic, onion, ginger, turmeric, rosemary, sage, oregano, cardamom and cloves can all add flavour and variety to a balanced diet. Some contain compounds with antioxidant or anti-inflammatory activity in laboratory research, but what happens in a laboratory doesn't automatically translate into measurable improvements in human lungs.\n\nOrdinary culinary amounts are generally less concerning than concentrated extracts, although allergies and individual restrictions still apply. And with COPD, eating well is particularly important, especially for those of us struggling to maintain weight or muscle mass.\n\n⚠️ A FEW PRECAUTIONS\n\nFood-strength herbs and concentrated medicinal extracts are two very different things. Herbal products can interact with blood thinners, heart medication, diabetes treatments and other medicines. Essential oils shouldn't be casually swallowed or put into nebulizers, and no herbal preparation should replace prescribed inhalers, bronchodilators or other medical treatment. If breathing suddenly becomes worse, that's a reason for appropriate medical assessment, not a reason to experiment with another home remedy.\n\n💜 MY FINAL THOUGHT\n\nI enjoy my teas, just as I enjoy my cappuccino. If ginger or chamomile helps me relax, comforts my throat or simply gives me five minutes of peace, that's already something worthwhile. But I don't expect it to repair my lungs.\n\nAfter all, if herbs could regenerate alveoli, the world's pulmonologists would be out of business and we'd all be growing peppermint instead of buying inhalers. 😜\n\nEnjoy your herbs as food, drink and comfort. Just don't confuse comfort with treatment. Stay safe and breathe easy. 💜\n\nGeneral information, not individual medical advice. Discuss medicinal herbal products with your pharmacist or doctor, particularly if you take regular medication.";viewArticles[14]._article2FullText=true;}

 if(viewArticles[15]){viewArticles[15].title="Did You Know?";viewArticles[15]._subtitle="Facts and Curiosities";viewArticles[15].text="🤔 DID YOU KNOW? 🤔\nFacts and Curiosities\n\nLiving with COPD, we spend so much time talking about inhalers, oxygen levels and breathlessness that it's easy to forget there are some rather curious stories surrounding our lungs. Some are genuine, some have a little truth behind them, and others have been repeated so often that people assume they must be facts. So here are a few worth exploring. 😜\n\n🩷 PINK PUFFERS AND 💙 BLUE BLOATERS\n\nYou may have heard these old medical nicknames. 'Pink puffer' was traditionally used to describe someone with emphysema who worked hard to breathe, while 'blue bloater' referred to someone with chronic bronchitis, low oxygen levels and sometimes fluid retention. They sound more like characters from a children's cartoon than medical descriptions, don't they? Nowadays we know COPD doesn't divide neatly into two such categories. Many people have overlapping features, and neither nickname tells us reliably how severe someone's disease is. They're historical labels, not modern diagnoses.\n\n🐕 CAN DOGS SMELL A COPD FLARE-UP?\n\nDogs have extraordinary noses, and trained dogs can detect certain changes in human scent. That has led researchers to explore whether they might one day recognise respiratory infections or other changes associated with worsening illness. But there's an important difference between an interesting research idea and a dependable COPD warning system. We don't yet have good evidence that Golden Retrievers, or any other breed, can reliably predict an impending COPD exacerbation. Lovely companions? Absolutely. Replacement for paying attention to symptoms? Not quite. 😏\n\n👉 WHAT ABOUT FINGER CLUBBING?\n\nFinger clubbing is when the ends of the fingers become enlarged and the nails curve more than usual. It's sometimes mentioned alongside lung diseases, but here's something important: clubbing is NOT a typical feature of COPD itself. If it appears, a doctor should look for other possible explanations, including certain lung conditions, heart problems and other illnesses. It's not something we should simply dismiss as another symptom of emphysema.\n\n🎭 ART, MUSIC AND A LITTLE DISTRACTION\n\nPainting, drawing, music and other creative activities can be surprisingly helpful when living with a chronic illness. They may reduce stress, provide enjoyment and give us something to focus on besides the next difficult breath. That doesn't mean a painting session will magically improve lung function, but improving how we feel emotionally is worthwhile in its own right. Personally, I find that a cappuccino, a good conversation and watching the world go by can do wonders for my mood, even if my alveoli remain thoroughly unimpressed. 😜\n\n🩺 BEFORE DOCTORS UNDERSTOOD COPD\n\nPeople have described persistent coughs and difficult breathing for centuries, long before anyone understood emphysema or had spirometry machines. Early physicians recognised that lungs could become abnormally enlarged or damaged, but the connection between smoking, chronic airway disease and declining lung function took much longer to establish. Today we have scans, lung-function tests and far better treatments, even though we still can't simply grow back destroyed alveoli. At least we've moved beyond trying to explain every cough with mysterious forces.\n\n😆 LAUGHTER — GOOD FOR THE SOUL, IF NOT NEW LUNGS\n\nA good laugh can lift our spirits, help us connect with others and sometimes release a little tension. But calling laughter a proven way to increase lung capacity would be stretching things. With severe COPD, a really good laughing fit can leave me gasping for air rather than feeling like I've just completed pulmonary rehabilitation! Still, I wouldn't give it up. Life's difficult enough without losing our sense of humour.\n\n🤗 MY POINT?\n\nNot every interesting COPD story is a medical fact, and not everything that improves our day needs to improve a lung-function test. A little curiosity, some reliable information and the ability to laugh at ourselves can all make this journey easier.\n\nWishing everyone a good, easy day. ❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing general information and personal observations, not medical advice.";viewArticles[15]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.82: Move the complete former Article 7 immediately before former Article 15.
 // Reorder after all position-based content updates, preserving each article's image, text and metadata.
 if(viewArticles.length>=15){
   const movedArticle=viewArticles.splice(6,1)[0];
   viewArticles.splice(13,0,movedArticle);
 }

 // V17.83: Remove duplicated title lines from the article BODY on the first three spreads only.
 // Keep article metadata, printed heading, index, poster images and every other page intact.
 for(let i=0;i<3 && i<viewArticles.length;i++){
   const a=viewArticles[i];
   if(!a || !a.text) continue;
   const lines=String(a.text).split('\n');
   if(i===0){
     // Introduction originally begins with two decorative title lines.
     if(lines.length>2 && /GREETINGS|𝗚𝗥𝗘𝗘𝗧𝗜𝗡𝗚𝗦/i.test(lines[0])) lines.splice(0,2);
   }else{
     // The first line is the article's duplicate decorated title, not body text.
     const first=(lines[0]||'').normalize('NFKC').replace(/[\p{Extended_Pictographic}\uFE0F]/gu,'').trim();
     const title=String(a.title||'').normalize('NFKC').replace(/[\p{Extended_Pictographic}\uFE0F]/gu,'').trim();
     if(first && title && (first.toLowerCase().includes(title.toLowerCase()) || title.toLowerCase().includes(first.toLowerCase()))) lines.shift();
   }
   while(lines.length && !lines[0].trim()) lines.shift();
   a.text=lines.join('\n');
 }

 // V17.84: Correct only Article 5 body text; keep its title, poster and all other pages.
 if(viewArticles[4]){viewArticles[4].text="𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗 is an umbrella term that includes chronic bronchitis and emphysema.\nCOPD and asthma are both chronic respiratory conditions, but they have key differences.\n\n𝗖𝗔𝗨𝗦𝗘:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Often caused by long-term exposure to irritants like smoking or air pollution.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: The most common cause of emphysema is long-term smoking, but it can also result from exposure to air pollution, secondhand smoke, and certain genetic factors.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: The primary cause of chronic bronchitis is long-term exposure to irritants, typically cigarette smoke or environmental pollutants. This exposure leads to chronic inflammation and thickening of the bronchial tubes.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Usually caused by a combination of genetic and environmental factors, including allergies.\n\n𝗦𝗬𝗠𝗣𝗧𝗢𝗠𝗦;\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Symptoms include chronic cough, excessive mucus production, shortness of breath, and wheezing.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Symptoms typically include shortness of breath, coughing, wheezing, and chest tightness. These symptoms gradually worsen over time and can severely impact a person's quality of life.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Common symptoms include a persistent cough with mucus production (often lasting for at least three months in two consecutive years), shortness of breath, wheezing, chest discomfort, and fatigue.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Symptoms include recurrent wheezing, coughing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath, often triggered by allergens or irritants.\n\n𝗢𝗡𝗦𝗘𝗧:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Typically develops in adults, often after years of exposure to risk factors.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: often develops over many years of exposure to the causative factors, such as smoking. Symptoms typically appear after significant lung damage has occurred, usually in middle-aged or older individuals.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Chronic bronchitis usually develops gradually over time, often as a result of years of exposure to irritants. It's more common in individuals who smoke or have a history of smoking.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Can develop at any age, even in childhood.\n\n𝗥𝗘𝗩𝗘𝗥𝗦𝗜𝗕𝗜𝗟𝗜𝗧𝗬:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Generally, lung damage is not reversible, and airflow obstruction is persistent.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Unfortunately, emphysema is not reversible. The damage to the lung tissue is permanent and irreversible. However, further progression of the disease can be slowed or halted with appropriate treatment and lifestyle changes.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Unlike some other lung conditions, chronic bronchitis is not typically reversible. The damage to the airways and lung tissue is often permanent. However, early intervention and lifestyle changes can slow down its progression.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Often, airflow obstruction is reversible, either spontaneously or with medication.\n\n𝗧𝗥𝗘𝗔𝗧𝗠𝗘𝗡𝗧:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Treatment aims to manage symptoms, improve lung function, and prevent exacerbations. It includes bronchodilators, inhaled corticosteroids, and lifestyle changes.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Treatment aims to manage symptoms and slow the progression of emphysema. This may include quitting smoking, using bronchodilators to open airways, pulmonary rehabilitation, and oxygen therapy in severe cases. Lung transplantation is an option for some individuals.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Treatment aims to relieve symptoms and improve the patient's quality of life. This can involve lifestyle changes such as quitting smoking, avoiding irritants, and getting vaccinations (like the flu and pneumonia vaccines). Medications like bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids may be prescribed to help manage symptoms..In severe cases, oxygen therapy or pulmonary rehabilitation programs might be recommended.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Treatment focuses on controlling inflammation and preventing attacks. Medications include bronchodilators, inhaled corticosteroids, and allergen avoidance.\n\n𝗟𝗨𝗡𝗚 𝗙𝗨𝗡𝗖𝗧𝗜𝗢𝗡:\n𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗: Characterized by a gradual, irreversible decline in lung function over time.\n\n𝗘𝗺𝗽𝗵𝘆𝘀𝗲𝗺𝗮: Significantly impairs lung function. It reduces the ability of the lungs to expand and contract effectively, leading to reduced oxygen exchange. Lung function tests, such as spirometry, can measure the severity of this impairment.\n𝗖𝗵𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗶𝗰 𝗕𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗶𝘀: Impairs lung function by reducing the efficiency of air exchange in the bronchial tubes. Over time, this can lead to decreased lung capacity and difficulty breathing. Lung function can be assessed through tests like spirometry, which measures the volume and flow of air during inhalation and exhalation.\n\n𝗔𝘀𝘁𝗵𝗺𝗮: Lung function can fluctuate, and most people with asthma have normal lung function between attacks.";viewArticles[4]._article2FullText=true;}

 // V17.81: visual heading normalization, restricted to written article headings and index titles.
 const uniformHeading=(value)=>String(value||'')
   .normalize('NFKC')
   .replace(/[\p{Extended_Pictographic}\uFE0E\uFE0F\u200D]/gu,'')
   .replace(/[\u{1F3FB}-\u{1F3FF}]/gu,'')
   .replace(/^[\s—–:•·-]+|[\s—–:•·-]+$/g,'')
   .replace(/\s+/g,' ').trim().toUpperCase();
 // V17.94: remove a duplicate title only when it is the opening line of the article body.
 // The underlying saved text is not modified.
 const articleBodyWithoutRepeatedTitle=(article)=>{
   const body=String(article.text||'');
   const lines=body.split(/\r?\n/);
   const normalized=(v)=>String(v||'').normalize('NFKC').replace(/[\p{Extended_Pictographic}\uFE0E\uFE0F\u200D]/gu,'').replace(/[^\p{L}\p{N}]+/gu,' ').trim().toLowerCase();
   const heading=normalized(article.title);
   let first=lines.findIndex(line=>line.trim());
   if(first<0 || !heading)return body;
   const opening=normalized(lines[first]);
   if(opening===heading){lines.splice(first,1);while(first<lines.length&&!lines[first].trim())lines.splice(first,1);return lines.join('\n');}
   return body;
 };
 // V17.85: Reorder the first sixteen articles only, using V17.84 numbering.
 // Requested leading order: 1, 2, 3, 11, 13, 10, 8; all others keep relative order.
 if(viewArticles.length>=16){
   const firstSixteen=viewArticles.slice(0,16);
   const leading=[1,2,3,11,13,10,8];
   const remaining=Array.from({length:16},(_,i)=>i+1).filter(n=>!leading.includes(n));
   viewArticles.splice(0,16,...[...leading,...remaining].map(n=>firstSixteen[n-1]));
 }
 // V17.86: move the current Article 11 directly before Article 5; preserve Articles 1-4.
 if(viewArticles.length>=11){
   const movedArticle=viewArticles.splice(10,1)[0];
   viewArticles.splice(4,0,movedArticle);
 }
 // V17.87: move V17.86 Article 11 after Article 8; swap V17.86 Articles 9 and 10.
